@*
    The page frame every feature shares: measure, header, body and the mobile action rail.

    WHY THIS EXISTS. Before it, the five feature pages used four different column
    recipes for the same job — `col-lg-7 col-md-9` (Regeneration, Meme),
    `col-lg-8` (Rap Roast results, which did not even match its own setup column),
    `col-lg-5` + `col-lg-7` (Style Director) and `col-lg-4 col-xl-3` + `col-lg-8 col-xl-9`
    (Bulk Generate). Nothing chose those numbers; they accreted. Each page then set its
    own `container py-4` on top of the layout's `.content px-4 py-4`, so every screen
    paid for its top padding twice.

    A measure is a decision about reading, not a column count, so this component names
    the three the app actually has and lets the pages pick one by intent.
*@
<div class="feature-shell feature-shell--@Measure.ToString().ToLowerInvariant()">

    <FeatureLayout IconClass="@IconClass"
                   Title="@Title"
                   Description="@Description"
                   ModelBadge="@ModelBadge"
                   Steps="@Steps" />

    @if (Aside is not null)
    {
        @* Split: a setup rail beside the workspace. The rail is first in the DOM, so it is
           also first in the tab order and first on a narrow screen — which is correct, because
           on both you configure before you look at output. *@
        <div class="feature-shell__split">
            <div class="feature-shell__rail">@Aside</div>
            <div class="feature-shell__work">@ChildContent</div>
        </div>
    }
    else
    {
        <div class="feature-shell__body">@ChildContent</div>
    }
</div>

@if (StickyAction is not null)
{
    @*
        The primary action, pinned above the fold on small screens.

        This pattern already existed and only Bulk Generate used it, so on the other four
        pages the one button the page is FOR scrolled away the moment you opened the
        options below it. It is part of the shell now, which is the only way that stays
        true for the next feature too. `aria-hidden` is deliberately NOT set: the rail is
        the same button, and hiding it from assistive tech on the viewport where it is the
        only copy would remove the action entirely.
    *@
    <div class="mobile-sticky-cta">@StickyAction</div>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>How wide the page reads. Not a column count — a decision about reading.</summary>
    public enum FeatureMeasure
    {
        /// <summary>One centred column. Upload, choose, look at one result.</summary>
        Focused,

        /// <summary>A setup rail beside a workspace. Use with <see cref="Aside"/>.</summary>
        Split,

        /// <summary>Full board width. Grids of output that benefit from every pixel.</summary>
        Board,
    }

    [Parameter] public FeatureMeasure Measure { get; set; } = FeatureMeasure.Focused;

    [Parameter] public string IconClass { get; set; } = "bi-cpu";
    [Parameter] public string Title { get; set; } = "Feature";
    [Parameter] public string Description { get; set; } = "";
    [Parameter] public string? ModelBadge { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public (string IconClass, string Label)[]? Steps { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The page body, or the workspace half when <see cref="Aside"/> is supplied.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Setup rail. Supplying it selects the split composition.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? Aside { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Primary action, repeated in a fixed rail below the md breakpoint.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? StickyAction { get; set; }
}
